WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.260
All it took was one erroneous letter&nbsp;
to end what seemed like a legendary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:09.167
"Jeopardy!" championship run in the making.&nbsp;
Some fans think Ben Chan got a raw deal.

00:00:09.167 --> 00:00:13.680
There's a reason "Jeopardy!" has lasted as long&nbsp;
as it has. Plenty of viewers tune in to test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.840
their own trivia knowledge. Others are invested&nbsp;
in the contestants, and there's no greater thrill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.280
than watching someone win one episode after&nbsp;
the next to get a formidable streak going.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.891
It's also tough to watch an impressive streak end,&nbsp;
especially when that loss comes with controversy.

00:00:29.105 --> 00:00:33.780
That was the case for the May 23,&nbsp;
2023 episode, featuring Ben Chan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.140
a nine-day returning champion.&nbsp;
He was in the lead going into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:41.604
Final Jeopardy, for which the category was&nbsp;
"Shakespeare's Characters." The clue was:

00:00:41.604 --> 00:00:43.699
"Both of the names of these 2 lovers in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.699 --> 00:00:46.140
Shakespeare play come from&nbsp;
Latin words for 'blessed.'"

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:50.700
The other two contestants incorrectly&nbsp;
wrote "Romeo and Juliet." The correct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:55.560
response was Beatrice and Benedick from "Much&nbsp;
Ado About Nothing." Chan was largely correct,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.820
but he misspelled "Benedick" as "Benedict."&nbsp;
According to "Jeopardy!" rules, that was enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:04.860
to label his answer as incorrect, and he ended&nbsp;
up losing the episode and his winning streak.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.940
"Unfortunately, that is not correct. The&nbsp;
correct response: Beatrice and Benedick."

00:01:09.394 --> 00:01:13.740
There have been plenty of embarrassing flubs&nbsp;
on "Jeopardy!" over the years, and Chan's was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.360
relatively mild compared to those. It's obvious&nbsp;
he knew the answer even if he didn't know how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.980
to spell "Benedick." Numerous people took to&nbsp;
Twitter to express their disdain over the ruling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:27.876
especially seeing how Chan is obviously a&nbsp;
smart guy who could've gone far on the show.

00:01:27.932 --> 00:01:29.221
One viewer tweeted,

00:01:29.221 --> 00:01:32.580
"Since when does being off by&nbsp;
one letter count in final jep?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.671 --> 00:01:34.711
There's no other character he could have meant."

00:01:34.711 --> 00:01:38.400
Many people agreed in the comments,&nbsp;
and it brings to light an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.820
distinction with Final Jeopardy.&nbsp;
Throughout most of any given game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:45.600
contestants have to say their response&nbsp;
out loud, meaning it just needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.181
sound correct. Final Jeopardy requires&nbsp;
spelling, which makes things trickier.

00:01:50.181 --> 00:01:53.160
Another Twitter user argued&nbsp;
the ruling was unfair in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.500
light of another contestant's response, writing,

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:01.064
"Lynn doesn't finish spelling 'Juliet' yet it's&nbsp;
ruled as a complete (albeit incorrect) response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.064 --> 00:02:06.000
and Ben misspells Benedick by one letter&nbsp;
and is ruled incorrect. Clearly they knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.900
what he was going for and yet ended&nbsp;
his run on a terrible technicality."

00:02:10.059 --> 00:02:13.479
Another claimed that Chan would've&nbsp;
been fine had it been a regular clue:

00:02:13.479 --> 00:02:17.220
"The thing I hate about #Jeopardy is if&nbsp;
Ben had said that response he would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:22.176
got it correct, but writing it was wrong.&nbsp;
The inconsistency has always bugged me."

00:02:22.176 --> 00:02:25.740
It may indeed seem like splitting hairs&nbsp;
to say "Benedict" is too far away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:30.120
"Benedick" to be accurate, but "Jeopardy!"&nbsp;
seems to be technically in the right on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:33.855
one. On the show's website, there's&nbsp;
a section on spelling, which states,

00:02:33.855 --> 00:02:39.060
"Jeopardy! is not a spelling test – unless,&nbsp;
of course, the category requires it. Written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.444
responses to the Final Jeopardy! clue&nbsp;
do not have to be spelled correctly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.444 --> 00:02:47.520
but they must be phonetically correct and not add&nbsp;
or subtract any extraneous sounds or syllables."

00:02:47.758 --> 00:02:52.558
"Benedict" is pronounced slightly differently&nbsp;
than "Benedick," so going by the guidelines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.558 --> 00:02:56.280
Ben Chan's answer should be labeled&nbsp;
inaccurate. And he may have been ruled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:00.133
incorrect if he said "Benedict" with an&nbsp;
audible "t" in a regular clue as well.

00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:04.453
On Reddit, one viewer thought the ruling&nbsp;
was tough but ultimately fair, writing,

00:03:04.453 --> 00:03:09.840
"Unfortunately as much as it looks harsh, it is&nbsp;
the correct call. I have to lean with the judges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.060
on this one. I just wish [host] Mayim would have&nbsp;
demonstrated the difference of the two names a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:21.060
more or explained why Ben's answer is wrong. Like&nbsp;
along the lines of 'Unfortunately the judges will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.956
not accept it as it is pronounced differently[,]'&nbsp;
or exaggerated the pronunciation slightly."

00:03:25.956 --> 00:03:27.705
"It's a very memorable miss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.705 --> 00:03:30.780
right? So if you're going to go out on&nbsp;
a miss, go out on a memorable miss."

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:35.940
Based on the online outrage, it's obvious&nbsp;
Chan was a fan favorite, but at least he's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:43.440
walking away empty-handed. His nine consecutive&nbsp;
victories earned him $252,600 in winnings. Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.593
he'll have a chance to win even more&nbsp;
during the 2023 Tournament of Champions.

